# RespawnPack .gitignore
#
# Philosophy: this repo ships the product. Anything that is build-scaffolding,
# a runtime artifact, or machine-local stays out of git so the tree a user clones
# is exactly what the pack is meant to distribute. If you fork RespawnPack for
# your own use, adjust these rules freely; they encode this project's own workflow.

# --- Dependencies ---
# Restored by `npm install` from package.json + the lockfile. Committing it would
# bloat every diff with vendored code that isn't RespawnPack's own.
node_modules/

# --- RespawnPack runtime / installer-generated files ---
# These are created by install.js inside a TARGET repo. They belong to the
# project that installed RespawnPack, never to this repo.
#
.respawnpack/
respawnpack.config.json
# Exception: the reference example under examples/ ships its filled-in config on
# purpose, to show a configured config next to the placeholder the installer writes.
!examples/**/respawnpack.config.json
# Exception: the PACK'S OWN root config, which is tracked because RespawnPack now
# dogfoods its own killed-feature contract. The rule above assumed a config only
# ever appears inside a target; that stopped being true the day this repo declared
# state.removals against itself. ⛔ IGNORING THIS FILE IS NOT A NEUTRAL DEFAULT —
# docs/derived/state/removals.json is tracked, so a clone without the config has
# seven removal rows and nothing scanning them, which kernel/lib/removals.js reports
# as FAIL, not CANNOT_DETERMINE. The two files are a pair and must travel together.
!/respawnpack.config.json

# --- Build-scaffolding for working ON the pack ---
# Not part of the shipped product: the maintainer's own local status/continuation notes and
# design scratchpad while developing RespawnPack itself. A user who installs the pack has no
# use for either, so neither ever ships.
RESPAWN.md
brainstorming.md

# --- Memory engine runtime index ---
# The memory engine's markdown files in git are the source of truth; `.index/`
# (in any of these locations) is a derived, rebuildable retrieval index. It is
# also machine-local (embeddings + a local PGLite database), so committing it
# would both bloat the repo and go stale the moment another machine rebuilds it.
.index/
memory/.index/
memory/**/.index/

# --- OS files ---
# Filesystem metadata the OS writes into every folder it touches; never
# meaningful to the project.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Editor / IDE ---
# Per-developer editor state and swap files. Everyone's local setup differs;
# none of it belongs in a shared repo.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# --- Logs & temp ---
# Ephemeral output from running the pack's own tools locally.
*.log
*.tmp

# --- Research working notes (top-level research/ ONLY — docs/research/ is tracked and ships) ---
# research/ holds the maintainer's working notes derived from external sources, and cloned
# third-party repos under research/prior-art/. That material informs the pack's ORIGINAL
# standards and skills (which do ship, with credits), but never ships itself, so the published
# tree carries only the pack's own expression.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash: an unanchored "research/" also swallowed docs/research/,
# silently blocking new research docs from being committed.
/research/

# --- Graphify (evaluated + wrapped, never absorbed; see ops/mcp/graphify/SKILL.base.md) ---
# graphify-8/ is a vendored upstream copy kept for reference during the bind-layer work; it is
# never part of the shipped pack, so it stays untracked rather than committed alongside our code.
graphify-8/
# graphify-out/ is Graphify's own build artifact (graph.json + caches) from running `extract`/
# `cluster-only` against a target repo. It is a disposable, rebuildable index, never source of
# truth (see the skill's invariants), so it never belongs in git.
graphify-out/
